    "long_instructions": "## Do This:\r\n\r\nImprove the pet adoption app to better support your assigned user.\r\n\r\n1. Adjust **up to 3 settings** using the control panel.\r\n2. Write down your changes and reasoning in your [activity guide](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1ZJ1TXv9EEma3mcTE9wVZy1fbiKxrlV8qy9Xb9lSl7xE/edit?usp=sharing).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n- **Setting adjusted:** Text size  \r\n- **Rationale:** The original text was very small and hard to read.  \r\n- **How it supports the user:** Larger text makes it easier to scan the pet details and reduces eye strain.  \r\n- **Potential tradeoff:** Larger text means fewer cards fit on the screen at once, so users may need to scroll more.",
    "ai_tutor_prompt_settings": {
      "answerTypes": [
        "ask"
      ],
      "answerTypeCustomizations": {
      }
    },
    "teacher_markdown": "- **Limit changes to purposeful adjustments.**\r\nStudents may change up to three settings. Push them to choose settings that directly address a real usability issue for their assigned user.\r\n\r\n- **Require profile-based reasoning.**\r\nEach adjustment must clearly connect to a need in the user profile. If a student says, “I made it bigger,” ask, “Why does your user need that?”\r\n\r\n- **Watch for aesthetic-only thinking.**\r\nIf students focus on “making it look better,” redirect to usability. Ask: “What problem did this solve?” and “How does this improve clarity or access?”\r\n\r\n- **Press on tradeoffs.**\r\nStudents must identify one downside per change. Encourage thinking beyond surface-level answers. For example, larger text may reduce information density; increased spacing may increase scrolling.\r\n\r\n- **Encourage balanced reasoning.**\r\nHighlight that a change that helps one user may create friction for another. Use this to reinforce that design involves decisions and compromises.\r\n\r\n- **Push precision in explanations.**\r\nResponses should complete the sentence frame meaningfully: “This helps my user by making it easier to ___.” Avoid vague statements like “It’s clearer” or “It looks nicer.”\r\n\r\n- **Surface reflection at the end.**\r\nDuring closing questions (“Which change helped most?” / “Which created the biggest tradeoff?”), look for students comparing impact rather than repeating earlier answers.\r\n\r\n- **Extend thinking toward user control.**\r\nWhen students answer “What user control could make this design work for more people?”, guide them toward adjustable settings (e.g., text size toggles, contrast modes) rather than one-size-fits-all fixes.",
    "offer_browser_tts": "false",
    "require_edit_to_continue": "false",
